33
00:02:38,033 --> 00:02:41,316
I keep hearing about container design.

34
00:02:41,316 --> 00:02:42,958
What is it, really?

35
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:49,106
A container that works is not automatically a container that is easy to run.

36
00:02:49,106 --> 00:02:56,335
Production platforms (Docker hosts, Kubernetes, ECS) expect every container to follow a few rules:

37
00:02:56,335 --> 00:03:00,097
one concern per container, logs on standard output,

38
00:03:00,097 --> 00:03:07,548
no important state inside the container, configuration from outside, and a clean shutdown on SIGTERM.

39
00:03:07,548 --> 00:03:11,015
A container that follows them can be restarted,

40
00:03:11,015 --> 00:03:15,809
replaced, scaled and debugged by anyone, with the standard tools.

41
00:03:17,567 --> 00:03:21,316
The official Nginx image is a good example of the rules.

42
00:03:21,316 --> 00:03:29,686
Nginx normally writes its logs to files; the image turns those files into links to the container's standard output and error:

43
00:03:29,686 --> 00:03:33,971
So every request ends up in docker logs, without any extra tool:

44
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:39,698
So every request ends up in docker logs, without any extra tool:

45
00:03:40,233 --> 00:03:41,133
One concern:

46
00:03:41,133 --> 00:03:46,908
the container runs Nginx and nothing else (a master process and its workers):

47
00:03:48,667 --> 00:03:50,246
Let me try something.

48
00:03:50,246 --> 00:03:53,931
A (hypothetical) worker written the "server way":

49
00:03:53,931 --> 00:03:57,616
it writes its log to a file inside the container.

50
00:03:58,167 --> 00:04:00,821
The container is running and working:

51
00:04:00,821 --> 00:04:05,126
the log exists, but only inside the container's file system:

52
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:08,731
Write to standard output.

53
00:04:08,731 --> 00:04:15,145
When you can change the application, print instead of writing a file; when you cannot,

54
00:04:15,145 --> 00:04:20,141
do what the Nginx image does and point the log file at /dev/stdout:

55
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:22,613
Got it.

56
00:04:22,613 --> 00:04:25,364
What do I need to remember?

57
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:30,609
One concern per container; scale and update the parts separately.

58
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:37,876
Logs go to stdout/stderr; docker logs and log collectors read nothing else.

59
00:04:38,433 --> 00:04:44,848
Keep state in volumes or external services, so any container can be deleted and recreated.

60
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:49,334
How does immutable containers work?

61
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:54,074
An immutable container is never changed after it starts.

62
00:04:54,074 --> 00:05:00,781
No docker exec to edit a file, no package installed by hand, no "quick fix" on the server.

63
00:05:00,781 --> 00:05:06,519
Every change is a new image, with a new tag, that replaces the old container.

64
